# -------------------------------------------------------------- agent guard
# Rules for failure modes measured in pi sessions against a local thinking
# model (2026-10 thinking-level benchmark, 48 sessions: 6 tasks x 4 levels x 2):
#   * bash calls get a default timeout (LETS_CODE_BASH_TIMEOUT, 300 s; 0 = none),
#     named in the prompt section: pi's bash tool has none, and two sessions sat
#     19 and 38 minutes on a script that never returned;
#   * a reply the output limit cut off before any tool call is continued
#     with a nudge to act (up to 3 times): a server-terminated thinking
#     section can turn into plain-text reasoning that eats the whole output
#     cap, after which pi stopped with nothing done;
#   * after pi's own tests have passed for LETS_CODE_WRAP_TURNS turns (6) and
#     2 minutes, or for LETS_CODE_WRAP_MINUTES (5), pi is advised once to finish
#     if executed tests cover every requirement (LETS_CODE_WRAP_TURNS=0: never):
#     sessions that had passed every hidden test kept polishing for 20+ minutes,
#     one breaking a working solution; a firm "stop now" after an ignored note
#     is opt-in (LETS_CODE_FIRM_STOP=1);
#   * verification means execution: a system-prompt section tells pi to check
#     behaviour and derive test expectations by running code, not by
#     reasoning about it (benchmark thinking hand-evaluated Python in 58% of
#     hard-task blocks); finishing with source edits no command has run, or
#     right after an executed check failed, gets one continuation (up to 2);
#   * after 3 source edits with no test run in between, pi is told to run its
#     tests (up to 5 times): a refactor that broke a passing solution went 4
#     edits and 4 minutes before the next test run;
#   * dynamic thinking (--thinking-level auto[:<ceiling>]): off, raised to
#     the ceiling for a burst when a struggle signal fires (the same tests
#     still failing after 3 runs, more failures after a source edit, 2 failed
#     edits in a row, 2 syntax errors, an identical call with no file change
#     in between, 3 tool errors in a row, 40 turns without passing tests).
#     The burst carries its reason as a one-line goal and ends when that
#     reason is resolved, after LETS_CODE_BURST_TURNS turns (3), or when pi's
#     tests pass; a new burst needs a new signal; at most LETS_CODE_MAX_BURSTS
#     (8) per session. LETS_CODE_THINKING_REPLAY=<n> keeps only the last n
#     turns' thinking in the context (default: all, as pi does). No signal fired on the easy tasks; every thrashing thinking-off
#     session tripped one within 1-4 minutes.
# LETS_CODE_AGENT_GUARD_LOG=<file> appends one JSON line per guard action.
# Inert unless LETS_CODE_AGENT_GUARD_ON=1. Rewritten when its content changes.
pi_write_agentguard_extension() {
    local dir f tmp
    lc_ext_module agent-guard; dir="${LC_MOD_DIR}"; f="${LC_MOD_FILE}"
    tmp="$(mktemp "${dir}/.agent-guard.XXXXXX")"
    cat > "${tmp}" <<'TS'
// Written by lets-code (agent guard); regenerated on launch, edits are overwritten.
// Active only in a pi launched by lets-code with the agent guard on
// (LETS_CODE_AGENT_GUARD_ON=1). Each rule answers a failure seen in pi
// sessions against a local thinking model (2026-10 thinking-level benchmark):
//   1. bash calls without a timeout get one (LETS_CODE_BASH_TIMEOUT, default
//      300 s; 0 or off disables it). pi's bash tool has no default, so a script
//      that never returns (a self-written fuzzer, a parser stuck in a loop) idled
//      sessions for 19 and 38 minutes. The model can still pass a longer timeout
//      itself: the prompt section names the default, and a call stopped by it
//      gets a one-line hint to rerun with a larger timeout.
//   2. a reply cut off by the output limit before any tool call gets one
//      continuation that tells the model to act instead of reasoning on. When
//      the server ends a thinking section at its budget, the model can keep
//      reasoning in plain text until the output cap: one session spent its
//      whole 32K budget that way, made no tool call, and stopped with nothing.
//   3. once the model's own tests have passed for LETS_CODE_WRAP_TURNS turns
//      (default 6) and at least 2 minutes, or for LETS_CODE_WRAP_MINUTES
//      minutes (default 5), it is told once (advisory, never "stop") to finish
//      if its executed tests cover every requirement; LETS_CODE_WRAP_TURNS=0
//      turns the note off. Thinking sessions that had already passed
//      every hidden test kept refactoring for another 20+ minutes, and one
//      broke a passing solution while doing so.
//   4. dynamic thinking (LETS_CODE_THINKING_AUTO=<ceiling>): the level is a
//      ceiling, not a setting. Thinking starts off and is raised to the
//      ceiling only for a short burst when a strong struggle signal fires:
//      the same tests still failing after 3 runs, more failures after a
//      source edit (with the tests untouched), 2 failed edits in a row, 2 syntax
//      errors in real files (a broken heredoc script does not count), an identical tool call
//      with no file change in between, 3 tool errors in a row, or 40 turns
//      without passing tests. The burst carries the reason as a one-line
//      goal plus "state a hypothesis, then confirm it by running code" (bursts
//      under the neutral template instruction used ~1k chars of their budget),
//      ends as soon as that reason is resolved (the edit succeeds, the
//      failures drop, a file changes...), after LETS_CODE_BURST_TURNS turns
//      (default 3), or when the model's tests pass, and a new burst needs a
//      new signal. At most LETS_CODE_MAX_BURSTS bursts per session (default
//      8). Falling failure counts and single failures never escalate. In the
//      benchmark these signals never fired on easy tasks, and every thrashing
//      thinking-off session tripped one within its first 1-5 minutes; the
//      earlier "stay raised until tests pass" rule turned such sessions into
//      40-minute polishing runs.
//   5. after 3 source edits without a test run in between, the model is told
//      to run its tests. A refactor that broke a passing solution (22/22 to
//      14/22 hidden tests) went 4 edits and 4 minutes before the next test run.
//   6. verification means execution. A short system-prompt section tells the
//      model to run before it reasons (check behaviour by running code, not by
//      working it out in its head), to derive test expectations by executing
//      the real thing, to settle spec ambiguities once instead of revisiting
//      them, and to change files with the edit/write tools rather than
//      rewriting scripts. If it tries to finish with source edits that no
//      executed command has seen, or right after an executed check reported
//      a failure, it gets one continuation asking for a real run (up to 2).
//   8. final review (LETS_CODE_FINAL_REVIEW=auto|on|0; auto, the default, means
//      dynamic mode and only after at least one burst): when the model is about
//      to finish with its tests passing, it gets one turn (at the ceiling in
//      dynamic mode) to verify each task rule against an executed test. It is
//      verification only: a failing new test is checked against the task first,
//      and no bursts fire after it starts (in round 8 a review-written test with
//      a wrong expectation made the model "fix" correct code twice). Most hidden-test misses in the benchmark were rules
//      stated in the task that the model never tested (one rule alone failed in
//      8 of 19 hard runs, at every thinking level), and mid-debug bursts never
//      revisit the task statement.
//   9. optional plan burst (dynamic mode, LETS_CODE_PLAN_BURST=1): thinking at
//      LETS_CODE_PLAN_LEVEL (default low: a 2k budget and a brief-thinking template
//      instruction) for the first turn(s), until the first source file is written
//      (at most 2 turns), plus a prompt line asking for a rule-by-rule test plan.
//      At the ceiling a single plan turn thought 27-54k chars.
//  10. dynamic mode also bursts once when half or more of the tests (and at least
//      5) fail in one of the first two test runs: the draft is broadly wrong, and thinking off
//      then fixes it one failure at a time (one session took 29 turns to get
//      there before an unrelated burst triggered the rewrite).
//      Fixed medium's advantage over dynamic was up-front design: task rules
//      handled before any test failed, which reactive bursts never revisit.
//   7. optional (LETS_CODE_THINKING_REPLAY=<n>): only the last n assistant
//      turns keep their thinking in the context sent to the model; older
//      thinking is dropped. pi replays all of it by default, which was about
//      40% of the prompt by the end of a high-thinking session, so the model
//      kept re-reading its own discarded hypotheses every turn.
//  11. adaptive thinking (LETS_CODE_THINKING_BASE / LETS_CODE_THINKING_DOWN, set by
