# Tiers: what each channel gives, and how the user unlocks it

`@@T@@/bin/probe` says which are present. You never do any setup step
yourself: give the user the steps below, quoted, with the trade-off.

| Tier | Needs | Gives | Does not give |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 intents | nothing (Termux's `am`) | launch apps, open files, pre-filled WhatsApp/SMS/mail/share screens, deep links | the screen, taps, contacts; the user taps the final button |
| 1 termux-api | Termux:API app + `pkg install termux-api` | battery, clipboard, toasts, notifications, dialogs, location, contacts, SMS, volume, wifi, sensors, camera, TTS, share (all: `ref/api.md`) | the screen, taps |
| 2 shell | Shizuku + rish, or adb to the phone itself | read the screen (`ui dump`), tap, type, keys, swipe, `dumpsys`, screenshot | non-ASCII typing (needs tier 1's clipboard) |

Without tier 2 these fail for Termux's uid; do not try them: `screencap`
("Failed to take screenshot"), `uiautomator dump` and `content query`
(`app_process: inaccessible or not found`), `input` (needs
INJECT_EVENTS), `settings get` (INTERACT_ACROSS_USERS), `dumpsys`.

## Tier 1: Termux:API
Steps for the user:
1. Install the Termux:API app **from the same source as Termux** (the
   probe's first line says it: GITHUB, F_DROID or GOOGLE_PLAY_STORE). The
   apps must share a signing key: a GitHub Termux needs the GitHub build
   from https://github.com/termux/termux-api/releases, an F-Droid Termux the
   F-Droid build, a Play Termux the Play build. Mixing them fails to
   install or to talk to Termux.
2. In Termux: `pkg install termux-api` (the commands; lets-code's setup
   installs them when the user enables this expert).
3. Android asks for each permission on first use (Contacts, SMS, Phone).
   The notification list needs Settings > Notifications > Device & app
   notifications (Notification access) > Termux:API.

Trade-off: every script that runs in Termux can then use what was granted
(all contacts, sending SMS that may cost money). Grant only what is used.

Every command, with its flags, permission and caveats: `ref/api.md`.

## Tier 2, recommended: Shizuku + rish
Shizuku (open source, RikkaApps, Apache-2.0) runs a small server as the
shell user, started once per boot through wireless debugging, no PC.
`rish` runs commands through it from Termux. Needs Android 11+ and Wi-Fi
for the start.
Steps for the user:
1. Install Shizuku: Google Play (`moe.shizuku.privileged.api`) or
   https://github.com/RikkaApps/Shizuku/releases.
2. Developer options: Settings > About phone > tap "Build number" 7
   times. Then Settings > System > Developer options > Wireless debugging
   (or search Settings for "Wireless debugging"):
   on (Wi-Fi must be connected; "Allow" on this network).
3. Shizuku > "Start via Wireless debugging" > Pairing. Shizuku shows a
   notification. In Wireless debugging tap "Pair device with pairing
   code", and type the 6-digit code into Shizuku's notification. Then
   Shizuku > Start. It shows "Shizuku is running".
4. Shizuku > "Use Shizuku in terminal apps" > Export files, into e.g.
   `Download/shizuku`. Then in Termux:
   ```
   cp /sdcard/Download/shizuku/rish /sdcard/Download/shizuku/rish_shizuku.dex $PREFIX/bin/
   sed -i 's/"PKG"/"com.termux"/' $PREFIX/bin/rish
   chmod 700 $PREFIX/bin/rish && chmod 400 $PREFIX/bin/rish_shizuku.dex
   rish -c id
   ```
   (Android 14+ refuses a writable .dex, hence 400.) The first `rish`
   makes Shizuku ask whether Termux may use it: Allow. `rish -c id` then
   prints `uid=2000(shell)`.
5. Wireless debugging can be turned off again; Shizuku keeps running.

After a reboot Shizuku is stopped: Wireless debugging on, Shizuku > Start
(no new pairing), Wireless debugging off. Wi-Fi changes do not stop it.

Trade-off: anything that runs in Termux (scripts, packages, a model) gets
shell rights while Shizuku runs: read any screen, tap and type anywhere,
install or remove apps, grant permissions. Revoke Termux in Shizuku's
app list, or stop Shizuku, to close it.

## Tier 2, without an extra app: adb to the phone itself
Steps for the user:
1. In Termux: `pkg install android-tools`.
2. Developer options and Wireless debugging on, as in Shizuku step 2.
3. Put Settings and Termux side by side (split screen: the pairing dialog
   closes when you leave it). Wireless debugging > "Pair device with
   pairing code" shows `IP address & port` and a code. In Termux:
   `adb pair localhost:<pairing port> <code>`
4. The Wireless debugging screen shows the connect port ("IP address &
   Port", a different number): `adb connect localhost:<port>`, then
   `adb devices` lists `localhost:<port>  device`.

Each time Wireless debugging is turned on again, after a reboot or a Wi-Fi
change, it gets a new port and the connection is gone: repeat step 4
(pairing is kept; `adb mdns services` shows the new port). Wireless
debugging must stay on while it is used, and it goes off when Wi-Fi does.

Trade-off: the same shell rights as Shizuku for anything in Termux that
holds the adb key (`~/.android/adbkey`). While Wireless debugging is on,
adbd listens on the Wi-Fi network; only paired keys get in, but turn it off
when done and avoid it on public Wi-Fi. Compared with Shizuku: no extra
app, but it breaks on every port change and needs Wireless debugging left
on.

## Accessibility-service apps
Apps that expose the UI tree through an accessibility service (automation
apps) can read and act on every screen, including passwords, and nothing
reliable and open source connects one to Termux today. Not used here;
prefer Shizuku.
