# Test design techniques and oracles

## 1. Equivalence partitioning
Split each input (and output, and state) into classes the software treats
alike: valid classes and each kind of invalid one. One test per class.
Take the classes from the spec or from observed behaviour (error text,
limits in code), never invented.

Partition table, written before the tests (a comment at the top of the
test file is fine):
```
Input | Class | Representative | Valid? | Test
age   | 18-65 | 30             | yes    | happy path
age   | <18   | 17             | no     | test_age_below_min
```

## 2. Boundary values
Both sides of every edge: min-1/min, max/max+1; 0, 1, many; empty, one
character, the maximum length and one more; first, last and past-the-end
of a list or page; negative numbers; dates at month end, Feb 29, the year
change, DST switches, time zones far from UTC; floats near rounding edges.

## 3. Decision tables
For logic over several conditions: conditions as rows, the rules as
columns, the expected action per rule. Collapse rules whose outcome does
not depend on a condition, then one test per remaining rule.

## 4. State transitions
List the states, the events and the allowed transitions (a small table or
a list). Test every valid transition once (0-switch), the important pairs
of transitions (1-switch), and the invalid events in each state: they must
be refused without corrupting the state. Typical: a game (turn, check,
mate, stalemate, quit), an order (new, paid, shipped, cancelled), a session
(anonymous, logged in, expired).

## 5. Combinations (pairwise)
Many parameters with a few values each: cover every pair of values at
least once instead of every combination. Build the table by hand for small
sets; keep it with the plan.

## 6. Use cases and scenarios
The main success scenario, each alternative flow and each exception, end
to end through the real interface.

## 7. Data lifecycle (CRUD)
Create, read, update, delete for each entity the change touches, and their
cross effects: read after write, delete then read, update a deleted one,
two writers.

## 8. Error guessing: the catalogue
Null, empty, whitespace only; unicode (emoji, RTL, combining marks, `İ`
under a Turkish locale); very long; special characters `' " ; < > & | $ \``;
injection strings (SQL, shell, path `../`, template `{{7*7}}`, HTML
`<script>`); numbers: 0, -1, huge, NaN, decimal comma; duplicates; order;
concurrency and double submit; timeouts, retries and partial failure;
missing, unreadable or locked files; disk full; network down; clock and
time zone; permissions; interrupted operations (Ctrl-C, kill, crash) and
the restart after them.

## 9. Properties, invariants, metamorphic and differential testing
When the exact result is hard to know:
- Properties: round trip (decode(encode(x)) == x), idempotence (f(f(x)) ==
  f(x)), invariants (a sorted list stays sorted, totals add up, a chess
  position has exactly one king per side), conservation, monotonicity.
- Metamorphic: related inputs give related outputs (a search with an extra
  filter returns a subset; a mirrored chess position has the mirrored
  moves).
- Differential: the old version against the new one, or two
  implementations, on the same inputs.
Generate many inputs (a property-based library if the project has one,
else a seeded loop) and print the seed on failure.

## 10. Negative matrix
For every expectation, its negation: missing or malformed field, wrong
type, out of range, wrong user, another tenant's id, after logout, replayed
request, out of order, twice. Each result is recorded: boundary verified,
suspected bug, or ambiguous (ask).

## 11. Oracles: how you know the result is right
- The spec or criterion, quoted.
- A comparable implementation, the previous version, a reference table.
- Consistency (HICCUPPS): with the product's history, its image and
  claims, comparable products, users' expectations, its purpose, the
  product itself elsewhere, standards and statutes.
- Oracle strength: re-read the effect from its source of truth (the file,
  the database, a GET, a reload), not a message saying it worked.
- Every check must be able to fail. Bite check: break the expectation (or
  the code, in a temp copy), see the test go red, revert. Negative
  control: run the new tests on the base code; one that passes on both
  covers nothing.

## 12. Coverage as a signal
Requirement coverage (each criterion), risk coverage (each High risk), and
code coverage where the project has a tool (`pytest --cov`, `c8`, `go test
-cover`): look at the changed lines' branches that no test reaches. Never a
goal by itself: a covered line can still be unchecked.
