# Criteria into checks, and what counts as evidence

## Restating a criterion
Write each criterion as conditions someone else could check without you:
given (state, input), when (action), then (observable result).
- "Users can reset their password" -> C1a: POST /reset with a known email
  returns 200 and sends one mail; C1b: the link sets the new password;
  C1c: an unknown email gets the same response (if the criterion or the
  ticket says so; otherwise do not invent it).
- Split "and": "validates the email and shows an error" is two conditions;
  both must pass.
- Words that quantify (all, every, any, never, only, at most) need the
  edge: the empty case, the boundary, the error path, a second instance.
- Keep the criterion's own words. Do not narrow it to what the work does,
  and do not widen it with requirements it does not state.

## Ambiguous and uncheckable criteria
- Two reasonable readings: name both, judge the stricter, mark AMBIGUOUS.
  The caller decides which reading the author meant.
- Not observable ("the code is clean", "fast enough" with no number):
  UNVERIFIABLE, and say under Missing what would make it checkable (a
  threshold, a command, an example).
- A criterion that names something absent from the work (a flag, an
  endpoint, a file) is FAIL, not ambiguous.

## Evidence, strongest first
1. A check you executed on the artifact, whose output you read, with a
   negative control that failed.
2. A check you executed without a possible control (say why none).
3. An existing test you ran, after reading its assertions and mapping each
   to a condition. A test's name is not what it checks.
Reading code supports FAIL (missing, wrong branch, unreachable code, cite
file:line) but never PASS. When reading is all you can do, the verdict is
UNVERIFIABLE with the reading cited.

Not evidence: the claim, commit messages, the PR description, comments,
test names, a CI badge or log you did not inspect, "tests pass" without the
output, a test that also passes on the base commit (for a new behaviour).

## Test traps (look for these in the diff)
- A test edited in the same change: compare it with the base. A weakened
  or deleted assertion, a widened tolerance, a new skip/only/xfail, an
  updated snapshot: the test was moved to the code, not the code to the test.
- A test that asserts nothing that the criterion names: only that no
  exception was thrown, that a mock was called, that output is non-empty.
- Expected values copied from the implementation (the same formula on both
  sides) prove nothing.
- A test the runner never collects (wrong file name or folder, not in the
  config): run the runner and find the test's name in its output.
- Mocked away the very thing the criterion is about.

## Rationalizations to refuse
- "It obviously works" / "it is a one-line change": run it.
- "The claim says the tests were green": run them.
- "Running it is slow": run the narrowest test or command that covers it.
- "It probably meant the lenient reading": AMBIGUOUS, judge the stricter.
- "That failure is flaky / unrelated": re-run once; still failing is FAIL
  unless it fails the same way on the base commit.
- "PASS with a note": there is no such verdict. Either it passes or the
  note names what is missing.
