# Negative controls: proving a check can fail

A check is evidence only when it can fail. Before you trust a green check,
make it go red where the criterion is not met; if it stays green, it does
not test the criterion. Never do this in the work's tree: use
`@@J@@/bin/scratch-copy` (copies live under the temp dir; edit them with
sed, patch or cp, which is allowed there). Read results per test.

## 1. The base commit (best for a change on a branch)
```bash
base=$(git -C <repo> merge-base HEAD origin/main)   # or the brief's base
nofix=$(bash @@J@@/bin/scratch-copy <repo> "$base")
cp <repo>/tests/new_test.py "$nofix/tests/"          # the NEW tests on the OLD code
(cd "$nofix" && python3 -m pytest -q tests/new_test.py); echo "rc=$?"
```
| Without the change | Meaning |
|---|---|
| fails | the test discriminates the criterion: good |
| passes | it checks something already true; worthless for a new behaviour (fine only for "must not change" criteria) |
| skipped, or not collected | it told you nothing: fix the invocation |

## 2. The change reverted in a copy (uncommitted work, no base)
```bash
c=$(bash @@J@@/bin/scratch-copy <repo>)
git -C <repo> diff <base> -- src/feature.js | (cd "$c" && patch -R -p1)
```
Then run the same check in `$c`: it must fail.

## 3. Break the one line (mutation)
In a copy, change the line that implements the criterion (`sed -i` on the
copy: flip a comparison, return early, drop the call) and re-run the check:
it must fail with the expected message, not for another reason (a syntax
error is not a control). Run the check once in an untouched copy first: it
must pass there, which shows the copy itself works.

## 4. Commands and CLIs
Run the command with input that must NOT give the result: an empty file, a
missing argument, an invalid value, the case the criterion excludes. If the
criterion is "prints X for Y", show it does not print X for not-Y. Exit
codes count: quote `echo "rc=$?"`.

## 5. Output, files, generated artifacts
Produce the same output from the base copy and diff the two; the difference
must be what the criterion describes, and nothing it forbids.

## 6. Web pages and APIs
`curl -sS -o "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/body" -w '%{http_code}\n' <url>`; then grep the body for
the criterion's text or field. Control: a request that must not show it
(another path, a missing parameter, a logged-out request), or the base
build when it can be started. Start servers in the background, log to a
file under the temp dir, and kill them when done.

## 7. When no control is possible
Code-only claims, external services, a criterion about something that does
not run here: say "no negative control: <why>". A PASS without a control is
weaker; say so in its evidence.

Finish with `bash @@J@@/bin/scratch-copy --clean`.
