# The expert file format

An expert is one Markdown file, `~/.pi/agent/agents/<name>.md`, with YAML
frontmatter and `expert: true`, plus optional files under
`~/.pi/agent/<name>/`. Only the user's agent dir counts: a project's
`.pi/agents` files are never experts. `list`, `run` and `/experts` read
the files on every call, so a new or edited expert works at once; the
always-on line is built when a session starts, so a new one enters it in
the next session.

## Layout
```
~/.pi/agent/agents/<name>.md     frontmatter + instructions (the child's system prompt)
~/.pi/agent/<name>/ref/*.md      knowledge read on demand (the instructions name each one)
~/.pi/agent/<name>/bin/*         helper scripts the child runs
~/.pi/agent/<name>/setup         run once when the user enables it (only if it installs something)
~/.pi/agent/<name>/runtime/      what setup installs; .installed marks it done
```

## The parser
lets-code reads a small YAML subset: `key: value` (one line; quotes around
the whole value are dropped), `key: >` or `key: |` followed by indented
lines (joined with spaces), and a block list under a key (`  - item`
lines). No nested maps, no inline `[a, b]` for `brief`. Lines starting
with `#` are comments. A file that fails a check below is ignored
entirely (it is not listed anywhere), so lint before you rely on it.

## Fields of every agent
| field | meaning | limits |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | the name in the line and in `expert({name})` | letters, digits, `. _ -`, starts with a letter or digit; default: the file name. Use lower-case with `-`, and name the file `<name>.md` |
| `description` | one line; the child's prompt starts `You are the "<name>" agent. <description>` | required in practice |
| `tools` | the child's tools, comma list or block list | pi's: `read, bash, edit, write, grep, find, ls`; MCP tools as `mcp__<server>_<tool>`. None given: all tools. Give the least it needs |
| `team` | experts this one starts itself (it needs `expert` in tools) | comma list of expert names. The child gets one level more and its expert tool lists and runs only these; they start nothing. A lead and its specialists, as `tester` with `ui-tester` and `api-tester` |
| `thinking` | the child's thinking level | `off minimal low medium high xhigh`; default medium. Avoid xhigh on a local GPU |
| `role` | the mandate role the child runs under | built-ins: `readonly` (reads and read-only commands only), `reviewer` (runs tests and commands, writes only under the temp dir, no git writes or installs: reviewers and judges), `developer` (edits and runs in its working folder and the temp dir only, no git writes or installs: an expert that changes code), `tester` (the developer's, plus the expert tool for its team: the test lead), `researcher` (the reviewer's, and no Playwright CLI or its runtime from bash: the researcher expert), `plan`, `auto`, `ask`, `yolo`; or a role from your `mandate.json`. Default: the session's mode |
| `agent_dir` | the child's own `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` (own mcp.json, settings) | a directory under the agent dir |
| `skills` | paths passed as `--skill` | under the agent dir |
| `append_prompt` | a file appended to the system prompt | under the agent dir. With an empty body, only this file is the prompt |
| `timeout` | seconds before the child is stopped | whole number, 1 to 86400; none: no limit. 1800 or 3600 is usual |
| `requires` | a path that must exist for the expert to be ready | under the agent dir; what setup creates |
| `requires_env` | an environment variable that must be set | a variable name |

Every path is relative to the agent dir (or absolute under it). A path
outside it rejects the file. For an expert, a missing `agent_dir`,
`skills`, `append_prompt` or `requires` does not hide it: it is listed as
"not set up", and its setup runs when it is enabled.

## Fields of an expert
| field | meaning | limits |
|---|---|---|
| `expert` | `true` makes it an expert (not a plain subagent) | `true`, `yes` or `on` |
| `use_for` | the few words in the always-on line: `name (use_for)` | cut at 100 characters; aim for 6 to 14 words. The routing signal: see use-for.md |
| `keywords` | replace use_for once the line passes 900 characters (about a dozen experts) | cut at 40 characters; 2 or 3 words. None: the first 4 words of use_for |
| `dispatch_rule` | one imperative line in the main session's system prompt while the expert is usable: when it must get the task | cut at 300 characters. Costs tokens in every request: only when route-check shows the model doing its tasks by hand despite use_for |
| `job` | one line in `list` and in the enable dialog | default: description. Say what it does and how long it may take |
| `brief` | the template its caller fills in | block list of `- name (required): hint` or `- name: hint`; a bad item or a duplicate name rejects the file. None: one required `task` |
| `platform` | where it is listed | `termux`, or Node platform names (`linux`, `darwin`, ...), comma separated; none: everywhere. On Termux Node says `android`, so `linux` does not include the phone |
| `setup` | a script run with bash when the user enables it and a gated path (`requires`, ...) is missing | under the agent dir; see setup.md |
| `setup_note` | what the setup does, in the enable dialog | say what it installs, where, how big |
| `route` | `false` leaves it out of the always-on line: the model does not route tasks to it, it is run by name (a pipeline stage such as the factory's `developer`) and shown by `list` | `true` (default) or `false`; use_for is still checked, for `list` and a later switch |

## What the caller sees
- Always, in the `expert` tool's line: `<name> (<use_for>)` for each expert
  usable on this platform. About 15 to 30 tokens each.
- `expert({action: "list"})`: job, state and the brief template of each.
- `expert({action: "run", name, brief: {field: value}})`: a missing
  required field returns the template and nothing runs. A dormant expert
  first gets the user's yes/no dialog (print mode: refused with a note).

## What the child gets
- System prompt: pi's, then `You are the "<name>" agent. <description>`,
  the body of the file, then the `append_prompt` file if any.
- Task: `Brief for the <name> expert.` and one `## Field` heading per brief
  field, in template order, then any extra field the caller added.
- Its own fresh context, the tools listed, the session's provider, guards
  and mandate role, its working folder = the caller's. It cannot start
  subagents or experts. Its last message is all the caller sees, capped at
  50 KB.

## Ownership
A file with the line `# Written by lets-code` (inside the frontmatter) is
lets-code's: it is rewritten on every launch. A user's own expert must
not carry it. Deleting the line from a built-in keeps your edited copy.
Names already taken: the built-ins (`termux-device`, `reverse-engineer`,
`tester`, `ui-tester`, `api-tester`, `expert-builder`, `developer`, ...) and the starter agents (`scout`,
`worker`, `general`, `reviewer`).

## State
Dormant until the user enables it (`/experts enable <name>`, or the dialog
on its first run); remembered in `~/.pi/agent/lets-code-state.json` under
`experts`. `/experts disable <name>` makes it dormant again. `/experts`
lists them; `/experts <name>` shows one with its brief.
