# Setup, pinning, helpers and platforms

Most experts need no setup: instructions, refs and helpers that use what
is already on the machine. Add one only when the expert needs a package
or a tool that is not there.

## How setup runs
- lets-code runs `bash <setup>` (cwd: the setup's folder) when the user
  enables the expert and a gated path is missing: `requires`, `agent_dir`,
  `skills` or `append_prompt`. Without one of those, it never runs.
- Gate it with `requires: <name>/runtime/.installed` and write that file
  as the last step, so a finished setup counts as done and a failed one is
  retried on the next enable.
- Progress lines go to stderr (shown live). On failure, exit non-zero and
  print the reason as the last stdout line (shown to the user). On
  success a last stdout line is a note: shown to the user and put first in
  the result of the run that set it up (e.g. an app only the user can
  install). Print nothing there when there is nothing to say.
- 15 minutes at most. No prompts: it runs unattended, without a terminal.
- The enable dialog shows `setup_note`: what it installs, where, how big
  ("installs yaml 2.8.1 with npm into ~/.pi/agent/config-convert, 1 MB").

## Pinning
- Exact versions only: `yaml@2.8.1`, `pip install ruamel.yaml==0.18.6`, a
  git clone at a fixed commit (`git -C dir checkout <sha>`). Never
  `@latest`, a range, or a branch.
- npm always with `--ignore-scripts` (postinstall scripts have copied files
  into ~/.claude and downloaded drivers): `npm install --ignore-scripts
  --no-audit --no-fund --loglevel=error <pins>`.
- Install into `<name>/runtime/`, never globally, never with sudo.
- Write the pins into the marker (`echo "${pins}" > runtime/.installed`)
  and compare on the next run, so a change of pins reinstalls.
- No `curl | sh`. Download a file, check its sha256, then run it.

## Helpers
- `bin/<tool>`: executable (`chmod 755`), a shebang, usage in the first
  comment lines, a short result on stdout, non-zero exit on failure.
- Node helpers can load the runtime's packages with
  `createRequire(<name>/runtime/package.json)`.
- Prefer what the platform has: bash, python3 and node exist wherever
  lets-code runs.

## Platforms
- `platform: termux` for the phone (Termux on Android), `linux, darwin`
  for desktops. On Termux Node reports `android`, so `linux` excludes it.
- Gate when the expert cannot work elsewhere (no browser on Android, no
  `am` on Linux); otherwise leave it unset so it works everywhere.
